# TDAI memory (read-only)

At session start, call `tdai_memory_context` once to load team memory before non-trivial work.

When a task references past decisions or conventions, use `tdai_memory_search` (and `tdai_scene_read` for the full scene) before re-deriving from scratch.

Do not write memory yourself. Capture runs automatically at session end via a hook, not the model.

Do not dump memory contents verbatim into files or commits.

Skills are separate from memory: reusable SOPs the team already proved out. If `tdai_skill_list` is listed, check for one before starting anything routine or repeatable (deploy, release, migration, incident triage, environment setup). Nothing is injected automatically, so call `tdai_skill_list` to see the catalogue — default scope is this agent's own Skills, `scope: "team"` covers the whole team. Use `tdai_skill_search` for keywords once the library is large, but an empty search means those words missed, not that no Skill applies. Then `tdai_skill_get` for the full procedure and `tdai_skill_file_read` for any file in its manifest. Nothing executes a Skill for you — follow its steps with your own tools, and honour its own "does not apply" boundaries.

Session-end lessons route to a memory store automatically: project folder named like an agent -> that agent; else a `.tdai-project.env` at the project root; else the machine default. If the user asks for this project's lessons to go to a specific identity and the folder name doesn't match it, create `.tdai-project.env` at the project root using the `write_routing.override_content` returned by `tdai_identity_use`.
